#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "b10aaa3e-2ba4-51af-be3b-056d72692885" :question/math-academy-id "e-10889" :question/problem "![](images/23/23e7d18cc240e7cf3937d11fd6484c3cce1027c237c85430f69f17bf25f0f487.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is given above. Find $f'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "To compute $f'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=\\dfrac32.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in [1,2].$ Notice that $x=\\dfrac32$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/90/9067715198a510cc1399bb257352babb1ac093db1c80c611c2236c10b43607bc.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(1,2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{0-2}{2-1}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'\\left(\\dfrac32\\right)=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096376 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "9811f16e-75aa-5982-b97b-cc59b33fb7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10890" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6ae60cb8606fb75aa49251ad894ce6a56173d6d5310aba0018e6c6933d1b93fe.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y = f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find $h'(-1)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = f'(x) + g'(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(-1) =f'(-1) + g'(-1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-1$ corresponds to \n\n- the interval $x\\in(-2,1)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in(-2,0)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1c/1c6ff6240abc7b56f058547e29ef250514fd19723057a65089ef0fa6da0ec044.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in(-2,1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta f}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{1-(-1)}{1-(-2)} = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(-1)=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in(-2,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta g}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{-1-2}{0-(-2)} = -\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g'(-1)=-\\dfrac{3}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & ={f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "1fb2491b-f442-57e3-990f-65bf1270d847" :question/math-academy-id "e-10891" :question/problem "![](images/69/69dd5f35d420746ebecc4fc7b01f09f0be01ccbc9b02cc3987533be5c0ec58ad.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find $h'(0)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = f'(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot g'(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(0)=f'(0)\\cdot g(0)+f(0)\\cdot g'(0).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(0)=1$ and $g(0)=-1.$ \n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(-1,1)$ equals $-1.$ So, $f'(0)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ on $x\\in(-0.5,1)$ equals $2.$ So, $g'(0)=2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(0) & ={f}^{′}(0)\\cdot g(0)+f(0)\\cdot {g}^{′}(0) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096378 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5e929c25-68b1-5a6b-92a0-d12b90a112e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10892" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9cd56facb5599cc8f2ffd8de8ee152cfec47844fc96d3daddb42e59afb0c8a57.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find $h'(2)$ where $h(x)=\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\nh'(x) = \\dfrac{f'(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot g'(x)}{(g(x))^2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\nh'(2) = \\dfrac{f'(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot g'(2)}{(g(2))^2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(2)=1$ and $g(2)=0.5.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in(1,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, $f'(2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ on $x\\in(1,3)$ equals $-0.5.$ So, $g'(2)=-0.5.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)-f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{{(g(2))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (0.5)-(1)\\cdot (-0.5)}{(0.5)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09155d3d-b192-5bae-a605-135f81cb4f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238148} {:answer/id #uuid "2a27fa9b-f9eb-571f-9ad7-adb78ba23899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238426} {:answer/id #uuid "3e212e53-c68a-501b-9fcb-1d4edff07617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238597} {:answer/id #uuid "9b25ef98-e399-524d-a61e-1e8de15956dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239408} {:answer/id #uuid "bffb9468-e4e2-5fde-8305-8df58aeb2faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e212e53-c68a-501b-9fcb-1d4edff07617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238597} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a281e5c-a134-5ef8-b7b0-5dd10c9df750" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66a00b85-1c8b-5d71-a8f5-a30ecfdeb00b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104782" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2a11d5b84bdf66b6406f9465ba6a8bc8ecdf364bea2dcd6902034990a74ef0a9.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is given above. Find ${f}^{′}(-3).$" :question/worked-solution "To compute ${f}^{′}(-3),$ we need to find the slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ at $x=-3.$\n\nLet's consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ on $[-5,-2].$ Notice that $x=-3$ lies in this interval.\n\n![](images/59/59159512fa1b51477f171584a3717e2bf2774e8c940b59525fe478e1bcf6aece.png)\n\nThe slope of $y=f(x)$ on $x\\in (-5,-2)$ is constant and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{-2-(-5)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-3)=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21c62e13-678a-598d-b191-e515187394a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238348} {:answer/id #uuid "6a281104-3172-5207-85ac-d3523e9f06ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238976} {:answer/id #uuid "7523980a-3dc9-556f-a900-bd8d74b02fb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239067} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5b7892-34f3-551d-a7a4-706e26b36bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240143} {:answer/id #uuid "fc55fbbe-3f5a-500d-a655-ae7d80f5fb9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21c62e13-678a-598d-b191-e515187394a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238348} :answer-field/id #uuid "16fa28a0-1995-57c0-b2e6-fa809fab21ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6c1e529-59cb-55e7-b759-671e209b110e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104785" :question/problem "![](images/df/df4fed951351dfc1203ba6257f404a5efadbec37b9f11098af1fc36a7793b0c7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=g(x)-f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)-{f}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=2$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-1,3)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/81/814ce0fdce1428b3bd619caa5e8d09a828d2f2c52fd0b20b7f520562319fb848.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{4-1}{3-(-1)}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-1,3)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-4}{3-(-1)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={g}^{′}(2)-{f}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096805 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e41277-3773-5e50-b48b-3e07ec725687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238104} {:answer/id #uuid "1a00f49c-5c8e-5e22-91ce-65f418c55c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238271} {:answer/id #uuid "24144e35-1ecf-550b-9f0d-0e56bd4dd66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238373} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9a5995-a974-5e53-80dd-de5ec2c79e33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239121} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ad6ad5-505a-576a-9124-401f591be2c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24144e35-1ecf-550b-9f0d-0e56bd4dd66e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238373} :answer-field/id #uuid "88a678ca-db36-56d8-badf-56a0bf3dbb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d460086c-0f96-558c-85a2-2debf9c57ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-104787" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e02f873d2d583767e0b29e7ad57121e23e5c73716ce058ef6bed09b644b37bce.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)+g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=2{f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,1)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f6/f64bb3d174950f3c4ac26d7d65d0b3b7881761fa3fb2ddd166f3a23c4af7fa27.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-4}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{2-4}{1-(-2)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-1)=-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =2{f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1881b409-b1a8-5937-8228-549730fa4bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238263} {:answer/id #uuid "6b6827c7-05a6-5550-99f4-48fa5234b475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238985} {:answer/id #uuid "a182d329-e4e2-53ba-9f90-9c8b26076ccf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239461} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d3c8be-d267-5a28-89eb-3e4892180913" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239784} {:answer/id #uuid "e11de059-0ba0-587e-b3f0-03533f07de15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1881b409-b1a8-5937-8228-549730fa4bf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238263} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fd248ef-8f30-5d69-8f77-96405a52c5ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fefd43d0-bf25-5b03-a050-63e64e251f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-104795" :question/problem "![](images/61/61c9cb5fed816e028cf47e3d6b264a40a2fc4c04bc5d330e8662a8dfd8fa26e1.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(2)$ where $h(x)=f(x)g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)+f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=4$ and $g(2)=3.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-2,4)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,4)$ equals $\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot g(2)+f(2)\\cdot {g}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3+4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cbbfc29-c1f0-5cc0-a8cf-211a3769caf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094760} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a58192-3909-55af-866c-3ed915fc5db6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094761} {:answer/id #uuid "d4396829-44ad-5ca6-89cb-3ce661bd35ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094762} {:answer/id #uuid "eef1db51-db6a-5a47-a551-b15745b2d0c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094763} {:answer/id #uuid "26a251bc-507d-59c2-9bd3-024456b8186f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094764}] :answer-field/id #uuid "867d1b6b-8aa0-5380-8e4a-aee322fabc53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094759}] :question/id #uuid "d64c4f89-ca1d-5e63-91b5-049fdf9385e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-15663" :question/problem "![](images/28/28ab65439d4ee144f9928e3b5048045858d4207794f886622b0961893d98615a.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y = f(x)$ and $y = g(x)$ above, find $h^{′}(2)$ where $h(x) = (f(x))/(g(x))$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317564 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096806 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "36b05580-d22b-5ac4-b8a3-cd8d9629ef74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Product of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f47953-8cd5-534d-a6e7-d92d99687392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f47953-8cd5-534d-a6e7-d92d99687392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315887} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d76746d-f8db-594b-b595-1ffdb75fb65d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "982f2951-5071-5227-bbfa-016bc05059cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-344897" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7af6b87184781cf0f6a3132f375e7917bf4f4cd8ef8b0a4979ab949ac9d6b6c7.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=h(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(2)$ where $p(x)=f(x)h(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)+f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(2)={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(2)=1$ and $h(2)=-1.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(2)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $h(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $-2.$ So, ${h}^{′}(2)=-2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(2) & ={f}^{′}(2)\\cdot h(2)+f(2)\\cdot {h}^{′}(2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e62398c-c7f2-5a90-94bd-6534d22dd4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328169} {:answer/id #uuid "99b31e0c-b9b1-53de-af5e-060da1733789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328262} {:answer/id #uuid "a8518df8-be72-56e5-a662-44b7d46a18e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328392} {:answer/id #uuid "dc886477-bb24-5eb3-b05d-d4dc07641d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328859} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6183e1-ed84-51b0-b43f-2a95ee508630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8518df8-be72-56e5-a662-44b7d46a18e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328392} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef80b9f7-4df8-5e89-9334-106d6c34b0e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "432a3453-fc74-5b9b-8b50-79e9585f1cfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-48952" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e13e109301fca50036cbf933b2a7946f3f53397f39f5e249c0d64e1f6ae78ba2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})$ where $q(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{3}{2})-f(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot {g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(\\frac{3}{2})=1$ and $g(\\frac{3}{2})=-1.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $0.$ So, ${f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=0.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})=1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot g(\\frac{3}{2})-f(\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot {g}^{′}(\\frac{3}{2})}{{(g(\\frac{3}{2}))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 1}{(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096804 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0a16e7b6-2a09-5aeb-9a8b-ea8fb1d59807" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa7bece1-3d5b-557e-9fdd-574dd0f25251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095971} {:answer/id #uuid "da644b27-d6fe-5fec-b21b-5186fdf9a24c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095972} {:answer/id #uuid "ee48073b-a5d7-5262-b9ff-6e53fd8635b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095973} {:answer/id #uuid "0b765475-bebd-51fd-94d7-a9d55fbb570d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095974} {:answer/id #uuid "45086bb8-6387-5a20-8912-e92976bd8c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095975}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a0ea8c81-b5b1-5de1-b59e-aa87545b7110" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095970}] :question/id #uuid "8af564c9-d2a2-5081-ad7b-03f735d2e565" :question/math-academy-id "q-72974" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ec9b64e62efa9435f5d00126164a01628c86ab16a49f52b478e81a83e5b7d2a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y = f(x)$ is given above. Find $f^{′}(-1)$."}]}
